# {{.project_name}}

The supported delivery path is dev to UAT; follow
[`docs/uat-promotion.md`](docs/uat-promotion.md) after the dev release gate passes.

A governed self-service analytics agent over a **neutral, git-versioned
semantic layer**. The architecture follows the published playbook for
LLM analytics that actually holds up: collapse ambiguity into one governed
answer per concept, make that answer easy to find, and flag when either has
degraded.

```text
question
  -> runbook agent (clarify first)
  -> semantic layer FIRST  (semantics/semantic_model.yml, compiled to SQL)
  -> curated references    (knowledge/*.md via the router)
  -> governed row fallback (`query_rows`, declared fields + typed values)
  -> answer + provenance footer (tier / sources / owner / freshness / SQL)
```

## Why the semantic layer is repo-owned

The contract in `semantics/semantic_model.yml` is human-curated YAML,
validated strictly (`src/app/semantics/models.py`) and compiled to portable
SQL (`compiler.py`). It is not a warehouse object, so:

- it ships in the same diff as the knowledge docs and eval cases (CI fails
  on drift between them — see `evals/offline_checks.py`);
- golden answers verify **credential-free** against the versioned snapshot
  (`evals/data/seed_data.json`) through `FakeWarehouseExecutor`;
- the warehouse is a plug-in. Databricks ships in
  `src/app/semantics/executor.py`; any other warehouse (e.g. Snowflake) is
  an application-code implementation of the three-method
`WarehouseExecutor` protocol (`run_plan`, `query_rows`, `latest_loaded_at`)
  — no platform change, same compiled grammar.

The model never receives an SQL tool. The Databricks adapter retains each
statement ID, limits warehouse concurrency to eight operations, and cancels
the remote statement when its 60-second tool deadline or the 180-second
request deadline expires. Questions cap at 8,000 characters, row results at
100, serialized tool output at 32 KiB, total tool calls at 12, and generated
output at 2,048 tokens.

## Lifecycle

```bash
python3.12 scripts/setup_dev.py   # preflight + isolated setup
make check                        # lint, tests, tier-1 offline gate
python scripts/sync_dataset.py    # publish reviewed cases to UC
databricks bundle deploy -t dev
databricks bundle run release_gate -t dev   # seed snapshot, then tier-2 gate
```

Tier 1 (`evals/offline_checks.py`, pull-request CI, zero credentials)
validates the semantic contract, the knowledge cross-references, compiles
every golden `expected_query`, reproduces every pinned `expected_value` on
the snapshot executor, and scores the recorded answer sheet with the
deterministic provenance scorers.

Tier 2 (`evals/evaluate.py`, the `release_gate` job) runs the real agent
against the live warehouse, adds Correctness/Safety judges, enforces the
regression baseline (`--update-baseline` records it) and cost coverage,
and evaluates the native MLflow EvaluationDataset named in `ai-app.yaml`.
Each run records its dataset ID/digest alongside semantic-model version,
knowledge digest, model + judge identity, git provenance, token usage, and
verdict. The gate fails if the registered suite differs from the reviewed
`evals/data/golden_cases.json` source. The SDK's strict judge resolver runs
before warehouse resolution or agent construction, so invalid judge
configuration fails before live target work.

## External prerequisites (platform-owned, one-time)

Provisioned through the approved platform process, never by this repo:

- `CAN USE` on a small serverless SQL warehouse (short auto-stop) for the
  deploying identity; its id goes into the `warehouse_id` bundle variable.
- `USE CATALOG` on the target catalog; `USE SCHEMA`, `CREATE TABLE`,
  `MODIFY`, `SELECT` on the target schema (the seed job writes the demo
  star schema and the dataset sync creates the evaluation dataset there).
- `CAN_QUERY` on the chat model and judge serving endpoints.

## Tokenomics

Every answer carries a `TokenUsage` (main vs adversarial-review pass); the
release gate fails answers without cost accounting (`cost/coverage`) and
records `tokens/mean_total` / `tokens/mean_review` per run. The optional
adversarial reviewer (`adversarial_review` input, or
`AAI_ANALYTICS_ADVERSARIAL_REVIEW=yes`) trades roughly +6% accuracy for
+32% tokens and +72% latency — enable it deliberately. Endpoint-side cost
attribution rides the platform's AI Gateway request tags automatically.

## Growing the eval set

The shipped 14 golden cases are a floor, not a target. Harvest every
correction: when a user fixes an answer, add the fixed Q→A as a golden
case, add the gotcha to the matching `knowledge/*.md`, and ship both in one
PR. Grow toward dozens of cases per domain; pin values to the snapshot,
never to live data. Compare runs against `evals/baseline.json` and treat an
unexplained regression as a release blocker.

## Graduating

- Registered prompts with immutable versions and aliases: see the
  prompt-app template.
- Serving the agent over HTTP (Databricks Apps + MLflow Agent Server): see
  the agent-app template; this project stays job + notebook scoped.
